{
  "dom_id": "step-q340067",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Given that $f(x)=9{e}^{x}+ln\u20614$, the slope of the tangent line at $x=ln\u20612$ is{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $ln\u20614$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9{e}^{x}+ln\u20614) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln\u20614) \\\\  & =9\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =9{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=ln\u20612$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=ln\u20612} & ={(9{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=ln\u20612} \\\\  & =9{e}^{ln\u20612} \\\\  & =9\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
